WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
To see drift's scatter, you need many populations at once.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.600
Do real animals spread apart like the beads, with their average left alone?

3
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.833
From one bottle's frequency alone, you could not tell.

4
00:00:18.293 --> 00:00:19.293
Follow one line alone.

5
00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:22.473
It climbs, dips and climbs again.

6
00:00:23.753 --> 00:00:25.313
Was it pushed, or was it luck?

7
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:29.753
Peter Buri published the real experiment, with fruit flies.

8
00:00:31.093 --> 00:00:34.693
He followed two alleles of a gene for eye colour, the brown locus.

9
00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:37.937
Every line started at one half gold.

10
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:43.137
Each generation, he picked sixteen flies as the parents of the next.

11
00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:46.837
His first series had a hundred and seven lines.

12
00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:51.797
Every bottle was set up the same way and kept the same way.

13
00:00:52.837 --> 00:00:55.817
So chance alone predicts differences between the lines.

14
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.880
Now stack all the lines into one picture.

15
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.720
Each bar counts how many lines sit at each frequency.

16
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.460
At the start, every line is in the middle bar.

17
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:11.543
Generation by generation, the pile flattens.

18
00:01:13.123 --> 00:01:14.123
Lines drift up and down.

19
00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:19.283
Some lose blue entirely, some lose gold, and those stay at the edges.

20
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:23.317
By generation ten, the edges are filling up.

21
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:28.477
Now watch the white triangle, the pooled frequency across all lines.

22
00:01:29.737 --> 00:01:33.317
By generation nineteen, will it be up, down, or still at one half?

23
00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:41.363
Still at about one half.

24
00:01:42.763 --> 00:01:45.243
By then many lines had ended with only one allele.

25
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:49.643
Yet the pooled frequency showed no systematic change.

26
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:52.793
Spread without a push on the average.

27
00:01:54.053 --> 00:01:58.473
That is the pattern drift predicts, now seen in real flies rather than beads.

28
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:01.537
But how fast did the lines spread?

29
00:02:03.097 --> 00:02:07.317
Buri asked how big an ideal population would have to be to spread that fast.

30
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:13.340
Here is the spread computed for an ideal population of sixteen.

31
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.730
Buri's real lines spread faster than that, with more of them at the edges.

32
00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:23.130
From how fast his own lines spread, Buri estimated about nine.

33
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.350
They drifted as if each bottle held only nine flies.

34
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:34.473
That number is the effective population size, Faster spread means a smaller

35
00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:40.313
effective size. It measures how hard chance acted, not how many flies were in the

36
00:02:40.313 --> 00:02:41.313
bottle.

37
00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:44.713
So one line wanders, and tells you little about cause.

38
00:02:46.073 --> 00:02:49.853
Many replicates, side by side, show whether the average was pushed.
